---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import Capture from '../../components/Capture.astro'
import Command from '../../components/Command.astro'
import { doc, packages, piDoc, shot } from '../../lib/content'
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['included', 'What ships with Phosphor'],
  ['install', 'Install a package'],
  ...packages.map(({ id, name }): [string, string] => [id, name]),
  ['maintain', 'Scope and updates'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Set up pi extensions"
  description="pi is a small agent harness built to be extended. Packages add the tools that make the agent useful beyond code, and Phosphor installs and configures them from Settings."
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="included">
    <h2>What ships with Phosphor</h2>
    <p>
      A model and pi’s built-in coding tools are enough to edit code. Add packages when a task needs
      more: researching a library, reading a ticket, checking a dashboard, or handing off a side
      investigation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Phosphor ships seven extensions of its own: artifacts, the context breakdown, MCP status
      reporting, opt-in Headroom integration, malformed tool-name repair, a wrong-worktree path
      guard, and a reload between turns that brings MCP changes into running lanes. <strong
        >MCP status reporting is not the MCP adapter.</strong
      > The adapter is a separate install.
    </p>
    <p>
      A package can contain extensions, skills, prompt templates, and themes. A skill describes a
      procedure; an extension can register tools, commands, or a whole model provider. pi’s docs are
      the reference for all of it: <a href={piDoc('extensions')}>extensions</a>, <a
        href={piDoc('packages')}>packages</a
      >, and <a href={piDoc('skills')}>skills</a>. Phosphor’s own are in the <a
        href={doc('extensions.md#bundled-extensions-phosphors-own')}>bundled extension reference</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="install">
    <h2>Install from Settings</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open <strong>Settings → Extensions</strong>.</li><li>
        Look through Recommended and check the package’s source. Pick one and choose <strong
          >Add</strong
        >.
      </li><li>Watch the job output and confirm the package appears in Installed.</li><li>
        For a package outside the catalog, use Add by spec with an npm, Git, or local-path spec.
        Choose global or workspace scope.
      </li><li>
        Start a new session so pi loads the package. Claude Code, Web access, Computer use, and
        Subagents get their own settings tabs once installed.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <Capture
      image={shot('guide-extensions')}
      alt="Phosphor Extensions settings displaying recommended and installed packages with example data."
      caption="Install a package, then set it up in its own tab"
      provenance="Settings demo · example data"
    />
    <aside class="notice">
      <strong>Packages run real code</strong><p>
        Packages run with full access to your system. Being in the catalog doesn’t make a package
        safe, and says nothing about its future versions. Read the source and requirements before
        installing. Some tools call external services, need API keys, or cost money.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>
  {
    packages.map(({ id, name, benefit, spec, url, recommended }) => (
      <section id={id} class="package-guide">
        <div class="package-guide-head">
          <h2>{name}</h2>
          {recommended && <span class="badge">{recommended}</span>}
        </div>
        <p>{benefit}</p>
        <Command cmd={`pi install ${spec}`} label={`Install ${name}`} />
        {id === 'claude' && (
          <p>
            The way to spend a Claude Pro or Max plan in Phosphor. It needs the official Claude Code
            CLI. Add every Claude account you have, and a lane can move to another one when its plan
            runs low. The <a href="/guides/accounts/">subscriptions and accounts guide</a> covers
            sign-in, routing, and the end-to-end provider test.
          </p>
        )}
        {id === 'mcp' && (
          <p>
            pi has MCP support of its own. Phosphor’s MCP Connectors tab works through this adapter
            instead, which replaces the built-in support in sessions. Installing it doesn’t connect
            anything yet. Next,{' '}
            <a href="/guides/connectors/">add a connector, sign in, and test it</a>.
          </p>
        )}
        {id === 'web' && (
          <p>
            After installing, open the Web access tab and set up a search provider. Each provider
            has its own requirements. In a new session, try a quick search and a page fetch, ask for
            source links, and check the tool output.
          </p>
        )}
        {id === 'subagents' && (
          <p>
            Start a new session and ask the agent to split independent work. Each child shows up in
            the chat with its current tool and its result. The Subagents tab explains foreground and
            background runs and where each setting lives. Every subagent makes its own model calls,
            so it spends tokens and quota.
          </p>
        )}
        {id === 'computer' && (
          <p>
            Check the Computer use tab and the package’s platform requirements, then grant the OS
            accessibility permissions it asks for. Try it on a test window before letting it click
            or type in anything sensitive.
          </p>
        )}
        <p class="package-links">
          <a href={url}>Docs and source ↗</a>
        </p>
      </section>
    ))
  }
  <section id="maintain">
    <h2>Scope and updates</h2>
    <p>
      Global installs apply to every workspace. For a project install, run <code
        >pi install -l npm:package-name</code
      > inside that project. It writes to the project’s config. pi decides what to load based on scope
      and project trust, so a package being listed doesn’t mean a running session loaded it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use Update all in Extensions, or update one package by spec. From a terminal, <code
        >pi update --extensions</code
      > updates packages and plain <code>pi update</code> updates pi itself. A pinned package may need
      its version changed by hand.
    </p>
    <p>
      After any package change, start a new session and give the new tool a quick test. Look at the
      tool output, not just the reply. Installed, signed in, and actually working are three
      different checks.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={piDoc('packages')}>pi packages: install and scope</a> · <a href={piDoc('extensions')}
        >Writing pi extensions</a
      > · <a href="https://pi.dev/packages">Browse the package ecosystem</a> · <a
        href={doc('extensions.md')}>Phosphor extension contract</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
